        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b082cfa2-7444-5bfe-952a-5440bae1d70a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Hormones: Angiotensin, Aldosterone\n- Cells and Cell Lines: H295R, NCI-H295R\n- Anatomy: Adrenal cortex, Zona glomerulosa, Adrenal gland\n- Concepts: Vasoconstriction, Blood pressure, Mineralocorticoid hormones, Steroid hormones\n\nFacts:\n- Angiotensin is a peptide hormone that causes vasoconstriction and increases blood pressure.\n- Angiotensin stimulates the release of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex.\n- Aldosterone is the main mineralocorticoid hormone and is a steroid hormone.\n- Aldosterone is produced by the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex in the adrenal gland.\n- H295R, also called NCI-H295R, is an angiotensin-II-responsive steroid-producing adrenocortical cell line.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
